{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"BOOKKEEPING STATUS: already durable — no re-post needed. The retry landed while I was reading\nthe other lane's comment. Verified programmatically: 6 comments, with the reviewer's\n`{}`-vs-`None` catch and the half-assertion catch both present. I am not duplicating them.\n\n## The kill — final corroborating data point\n\nA live PRE-FIX sweep had to be MANUALLY TERMINATED to unblock the fleet: pid 98440,\n`compact_trigger.run_sweep` from the `pony2-richtext` worktree, 26 minutes elapsed, killed\nmid-hold at 201s into a single lock acquisition. Three agents' store writes were blocked behind\nit. `rebar doctor` shows all locks free afterwards.\n\nI experienced this from inside: my own `rebar comment` on this ticket exhausted 11 retry passes\nagainst a holder reported at 867s, and a later attempt produced no output at all. I could not\nreliably write to the ticket store while documenting the fix for why the ticket store cannot be\nwritten to.\n\nThat makes the evidence chain for this ticket complete and independent at three points:\n1. operator profiling (fresh `git log --full-history` child every few seconds at ~100% CPU);\n2. a different lane's session (`vigilant-erasable-pony`) blocked twice, once for 24+ minutes at\n   0.0% CPU — I/O-bound on git, not spinning — losing a `transition ... closed` AFTER its\n   completion verification had already been paid for;\n3. this manual kill, plus my own 867s write starvation.\n\n## What the other lane's observation adds, and it sharpens the diagnosis\n\nThe command they captured is:\n```\ngit ... log --diff-filter=A --full-history --no-renames --format=%H -- '*<event-id>*.json'\n```\nThat LEADING-WILDCARD, per-EVENT pathspec is `resolve_event_commit`'s\n(`attest/authorship.py:139` globs `<position>-*.json`), NOT\n`build_ticket_position_commit_map`'s directory prefix. So the deployed sweep is not merely\ndoing one walk per ticket — it is falling through to PER-EVENT walks for every position its\nper-ticket map misses, and a leading wildcard defeats pathspec pruning so each one is a full\nhistory scan. The real deployed cost is worse than the ticket description states.\n\n1739 covers exactly this: the whole-store position map is built once, and the test asserts\n`per_event == 0` and `per_position == 0` — i.e. it fails if ANY event falls through to that\nwildcard walk. That assertion was vacuous until I fixed the fixture to produce signed,\ncommitted events, which is the correction recorded above.\n\n## Two operability asks from that lane are NOT in 1739's scope\n\nThey ask that (a) the lock holder record identify the holder as optional housekeeping, and\n(b) an operator be told that killing it is safe. Both are fair — this kill needed `lsof`/`ps`\nto identify a holder that `run_sweep`'s own docstring already guarantees is safe to interrupt.\nNeither is trivial: the ownership stamp is the shared v2 format with documented forward-compat\nconstraints. Filing separately rather than expanding a change that is at LLM-Review +1 with\nVerified pending.\n\nExpected until 1739 merges: the killed sweep will be retriggered by the next close on any\nworktree, under OLD code. Not chasing it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786655621175623001,"uuid":"30a61c5a-1f7c-418a-84ce-a5c1ed6a8819"}